  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Heat will be the name of the game over the \nnext few days as the Bermuda high centered off the coast will \nslide S'wards towards the Gulf Coast States while a thermal \ntrough remains over the Piedmont. This will keep low level flow \ngenerally SW to W over the next several days. As a result, some \nweak WAA will allow low level thicknesses to increase closer to \n1430m. Based off the offices climatological study this is \nforecast to result in highs in the low to mid 90s across the \ninland areas and upper 80s to low 90s along the OBX and coast \nthrough much of the week before a front impacts the region \nresulting in cooler temperatures late week into the weekend. \n\nHeat indices are forecast to peak around 100-105 this afternoon,\njust shy of advisory criteria, but are expected to climb to\naround 105-110 Tue through Thurs, and potentially lingering \ninto Fri with heat headlines probably for much of the area. \nLows will be in the 70s which won't provide much relief from \nthe heat either. We encourage anyone planning on spending alot \nof time outdoors this week to take proper precautions and stay \nhydrated. \n   \nOtherwise, a typical summer time pattern will be in place through \nmuch the week. Near zonal flow aloft with a series of shortwave \ntroughs pushing across the area will result climo precip chances for \nthe next several days with best chances through peak heating and \nseabreeze related for the most part but will also depend on timing \nand exact location of individual shortwave troughs. The severe \nthreat appears minimal for the next couple of days with limited\nshear, though somewhat better shear develops toward the middle \nof the week. We do look to have increased precipitation chances \ncloser to Fri and into next weekend with the approach of an \nincoming cold front.",
